on '%s'\\n\", args[0])\n\t\t\t\tfmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, \"Try 'logname --help' for more information.\")\n\t\t\t\tos.Exit(1)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn args\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn args\n}\n```\n\n### /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5368/cmd/logname/logname_test.go\n\n```go\n// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n\n// Implements srd053-logname R3.1-R3.3.\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\n\t\"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils\"\n)\n\nfunc TestDiff(t *testing.T) {\n\tgoBin := testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")\n\trefBin, err := exec.LookPath(\"glogname\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Skip(\"reference binary not found\")\n\t}\n\n\tbinaryNameRe := regexp.MustCompile(`(?:/\\S+/)?g?logname`)\n\tnormalizeBinaryName := testutils.NormalizeFunc(func(b []byte) []byte {\n\t\treturn binaryNameRe.ReplaceAll(b, []byte(\"logname\"))\n\t})\n\n\ttests := []testutils.DiffTest{\n\t\t{Name: \"no_args\", Env: []string{\"LC_ALL=C\"}},\n\t\t{Name: \"extra_operand\", Args: []string{\"extraarg\"}, ExitCode: 1, Env: []string{\"LC_ALL=C\"}, Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normalizeBinaryName}},\n\t\t{Name: \"unknown_flag\", Args: []string{\"--unknown\"}, ExitCode: 1, Env: []string{\"LC_ALL=C\"}, Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{normalizeBinaryName}},\n\t}\n\n\ttestutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\n}\n```\n\n## 2. Full DiffTest Struct Definition\n\nFrom /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5368/pkg/testutils/difftest.go (lines 30-55):\n\n```go\n// DiffTest defines a single differential test case.\n//\n// R1.1: Name identifies the subtest (used with t.Run). Args holds command-line\n// arguments passed to both binaries.\n//\n// R1.2: Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately. An empty non-nil\n// slice ([]byte{}) also produces no bytes but represents an open stdin that is\n// immediately closed. ExitCode defaults to 0 when left as the zero value.\n//\n// R1.3: Env nil means both binaries inherit the test process environment with\n// LC_ALL=C applied as a default override. When non-nil, KEY=VALUE pairs are\n// merged into the inherited environment: matching keys are overridden, new keys\n// are added.\n//\n// R1.4: Normalize holds a slice of NormalizeFunc applied in order to stdout and\n// stderr of both binaries before comparison. Nil or empty means no normalization.\ntype DiffTest struct {\n\tName          string\n\tArgs          []string\n\tStdin         []byte\n\tEnv           []string\n\tWorkDir       string\n\tExitCode      int\n\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n}\n```\n\nThe struct has 8 fields total:\n- **Name** (string) - identifies the subtest\n- **Args** ([]string) - command-line arguments\n- **Stdin** ([]byte) - input to stdin (nil means EOF immediately)\n- **Env** ([]string) - environment variables (KEY=VALUE pairs)\n- **WorkDir** (string) - working directory for the command\n- **ExitCode** (int) - expected exit code (defaults to 0)\n- **Normalize** ([]NormalizeFunc) - normalization functions to apply before comparison\n- **ExpectedFiles** (map[string][]byte) - expected files to check after execution\n\n## 3. Supplementary Group IDs in Go - cmd/groups/ Pattern\n\nLooking at **cmd/groups/main.go**, the project uses Go's standard library approach for getting supplementary group IDs:\n\n**For the current process** (lines 51-64):\n```go\nfunc printCurrentUserGroups() error {\n\tgids, err := syscall.Getgroups()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tnames, err := resolveGroupIDs(gids)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif _, err := fmt.Fprintln(os.Stdout, strings.Join(names, \" \")); err != nil {\n\t\tos.Exit(1)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n```\n\n**For a specific user** (lines 66-91):\n```go\nfunc printUserGroups(username string) error {\n\tu, err := user.Lookup(username)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tgidStrs, err := u.GroupIds()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tgids := make([]int, 0, len(gidStrs))\n\tfor _, s := range gidStrs {\n\t\tgid, err := strconv.Atoi(s)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t\tgids = append(gids, gid)\n\t}\n\tnames, err := resolveGroupIDs(gids)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif _, err := fmt.Fprintf(os.Stdout, \"%s : %s\\n\", username, strings.Join(names, \" \")); err != nil {\n\t\tos.Exit(1)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n```\n\n**Group ID resolution** (lines 93-104):\n```go\nfunc resolveGroupIDs(gids []int) ([]string, error) {\n\tnames := make([]string, 0, len(gids))\n\tfor _, gid := range gids {\n\t\tg, err := user.LookupGroupId(strconv.Itoa(gid))\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tnames = append(names, strconv.Itoa(gid))\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tnames = append(names, g.Name)\n\t}\n\treturn names, nil\n}\n```\n\n**Key approach**: The project uses `syscall.Getgroups()` for the current process and `user.User.GroupIds()` for specific users, then resolves group IDs to names using `user.LookupGroupId()`, falling back to numeric IDs if group lookup fails."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa3fee76-1f98-465b-9d3b-deb0828dedfd","uuid":"ff40cc0a-c347-4e47-8b8d-e3bc11fe9a3b","timestamp":"2026-05-20T14:28:35.650Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand patterns used in cmd/ implementations in this Go project at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5368/. \n\n1. Find a simple cmd/ that has both main.go and a _test.go using differential testing. Look at cmd/whoami/ or cmd/logname/ or cmd/groups/ as likely simple examples. Show me the full contents of both files for the simplest one you find.\n\n2. Show me the full DiffTest struct definition from pkg/testutils/difftest.go - I need all fields.\n\n3. Check if there's a way to get supplementary group IDs in Go - look at how any existing cmd/ handles group lookups (e.g., cmd/